ing the opponent easy access to corners or stable edges.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Moves (A, 7) and (B, 7) are strategically poor as they correspond to C-squares and X-squares respectively, adjacent to the empty A8 corner, which could allow the opponent to take the corner.\n- Move (A, 5) captures a vertical line of 5 pieces. While tempting, flipping this many pieces early on is often detrimental as it reduces my future mobility and creates a large frontier for the opponent to attack.\n- Move (D, 8) captures an edge position. While relatively safe, it flips 3 internal pieces.\n- Move (E, 3) is an internal "quiet move". It flips only one opponent piece (F4) and consolidates my position in the center-left without exposing me to edge risks.\n\nReason: (E, 3) is the most strategic choice. In Reversi/Othello, making moves that flip fewer pieces (quiet moves) in the early game is advantageous to maintain mobility (options for future turns) and restrict the opponent\'s options. It avoids the structural risks of playing next to corners or taking unstable edges prematurely.\n\nAnswer: (E, 3)'}
