. Not terrible, but it leaves some frontiers.\n\n*   **(B, 7):**  Another X-square! Avoid!\n\n*   **(C, 7):** This one looks more promising. It flips 2 pieces and is internal/near the edge. Nothing too dangerous there.\n\n*   **(E, 7):** A vertical edge and it flips 3.\n\n*   **(G, 3):**  This seems the safest by far. It flips only one disc (G4), and it\'s somewhat "protected" in the center of the board.\n\n*   **(H, 4):**  This is another edge move, flipping only one piece (G4). It builds a piece structure on the edge, which is good. However, it\'s next to my opponent\'s piece at H3. *This is suspicious.* If I play here it invites opponent to flip the edge, which is not good.\n\nNow, I\'m doing a deeper dive to compare G3 and H4. G3 flips G4, which might limit opponent\'s mobility.  H4 also flips G4, which makes a strong edge.  But wait, if I put a piece on H4, and my opponent has H3 (@), this sets up a *complete* flipping scenario on the H column (If opponent plays H7). This could mean giving my opponent the whole edge! That is very very bad. **H4 is a trap!** So, I\'m eliminating H4 from the choices.\n\nOkay, let\'s go back to G3. Does G3 present similar problems? Does it give the opponent a good flip? I check all around. No, G3 does not create an easy flip, even if the opponent plays at H4, I still control it and prevent an edge sweep.\n\nSo, comparing G3 and the other moves: I\'m avoiding the risky edge plays, C-square, and X-squares.  I want to minimize flips so I have the most mobility. G3 is internal and quiet.\n\nTherefore, the best move in this situation is G3.\n\nFinal Answer: (G, 3)\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as \'*\' (Black) in the mid-game with 8 pieces against 16. The board layout shows I have a presence in the bottom-right center. I need to make a move that increases my stability and minimizes the opponent\'s opportunities. Several edge and internal moves are available.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(H, 4)**: This is an edge move. While it flips G4 and connects to my pieces at H5 and H6, it creates a dangerous liability. With H3 occupied by the opponent (\'@\'), playing H4 creates a continuous line of my pieces (H4-H6). The opponent can then play H7, sandwiching my entire edge group against H3, flipping them all and securing a strong edge position. Thus, H4 is a trap.\n- **(A, 5) & (A, 6)**: These moves flip a large number of pieces (5 and 4 respectively) on rows 5 and 6. Flipping too many pieces in the mid-game ("frontier expansion") is generally poor strategy as it gives the opponent many new valid moves and reduces my mobility.\n- **(B, 4)**: This flips 3 pieces and creates a horizontal wall on row 4. It also risks allowing the opponent to play A4, potentially sweeping the row back.\n- **(G, 3)**: This is a "quiet move" (internal) that flips only one piece (G4). It consolidates the center without creating weak edges or large frontiers for the opponent to exploit.\n\nReason: Move (G, 3) is the best option because it follows the strategy of minimizing flips to restrict the opponent\'s mobility. It is a safe internal move that strengthens my central position and avoids the devastating edge trap associated with move (H, 4).\n\nAnswer: (G, 3)'}
